> >5. To feed your dog/cat should you throw food at him, or drop food and
> >   walk away, or throw food next to him? Also when trying to train it
> >   to steal (I believe this is done by rewarding your pet when it
> >   picks some stuff) should you reward it when it picks something up
> >   or when it drops it? Should you feed it and then pick the stuff up,
> >   or vice versa? Should this be done only at shops or at other times
> >   as well? Do you reward your pet for fighting monsters for you?
> 
> The "rewarding" actually works when the pet eats non-corpse food, period.
> Supposedly it will be more likely to drop stuff next to you then.  This does
> not work too well, and you really don't need to worry about it.

Au contraire, it works quite well.  I usually play a tourist out of sheer
bloody-mindedness, but also because he starts with plenty of food, missile
weapons, and usually a fair amount of tripe for Fido.  I train my pet by
throwing a dart across the room, waiting for him to drop it on a square
adjacent to me, then throw a tripe ration at him when he moves off it.
I find that a pet I train this way is MUCH better in shops than an un-
trained one (found wild later, or started with no tripe).

Note that training works for dogs and cats both (a bit of anti-realism
there...).

